<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kong Empire</h1><p class="st-meta">1710 CE – 1898 CE (approx.) · capital Kong</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Dyula Muslim trading state in northern Ivory Coast, built by Seku Wattara around the city of Kong, a centre of commerce and Islamic learning.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kong-cote-divoire">Kong</a> was already a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangara">Dyula</a> market town when, around 1710, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seku-wattara">Seku Wattara</a> seized power from its earlier rulers and launched campaigns that extended its influence north towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-volta">Black Volta</a> and the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>. The Wattara (Ouattara) family became the ruling dynasty, and their armies relied on cavalry and imported firearms.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kong-cote-divoire">Kong</a> was more a commercial network than a centralised empire. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangara">Dyula</a> merchants traded gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kola-nut">kola nuts</a>, cloth and slaves between the forest and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a>, and the town had mosques and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Qur'anic</a> schools of wide reputation. The warrior estate, the Sonangi, did the fighting while Muslim scholars and traders ran the city. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seku-wattara">Seku</a>'s death about 1745 the realm fragmented and his relative Famagan founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-gwiriko">Kingdom of Gwiriko</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kong-cote-divoire">Kong</a> declined into a regional trading town. The French explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-gustave-binger">Louis-Gustave Binger</a> arrived in 1888, and a French treaty followed in 1889. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samori-ture">Samori Toure</a> sacked and burned Kong in 1897, and the area was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-ivory-coast">French Ivory Coast</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kong-empire.jpg" alt="Kong Empire" width="320" height="431"><figcaption>Image: HetmanTheResearcher · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kong_Empire.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1710 CE – 1898 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kong</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Seku Wattara</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dyula</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a>, <a href="/countries/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Kong, Ouattara Empire</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1804&amp;focus=kong-empire" aria-label="Kong Empire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4553 1836 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kong Empire lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4873 2092L4747 2063M4873 2092L5054 728M4873 2092L4882 2016"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4747 2063h0M5054 728h0M4882 2016h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4873 2092h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4873 2092h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kong. <a href="/atlas?year=1804&amp;focus=kong-empire">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

